id: k8s.job
kind: kubernetes
title: Job
aliases: job, batch workload
tags: workloads, batch
questions:
  - how do you run a batch task in Kubernetes?
invariant: A Job runs Pods until a declared number of successful completions is reached.
answer_30s: A Job is a run-to-completion workload: it tracks successes and retries failures until `completions` is reached.
answer_2m: |
  Unlike a Deployment, termination is success. Retry behaviour is the
  controller's job: backoffLimit bounds retries, activeDeadlineSeconds bounds
  total runtime, and restartPolicy Never keeps failed Pods around for
  inspection. Parallelism is declared, not inferred -- which is the part
  interviewers probe: a Job that needs an external queue, or an idempotent unit
  of work, cannot get either from the Job itself.
when_to_use:
  - migrations, one-shot batch processing, explicit retry accounting
avoid_when:
  - the workload must always be running
commands:
  - kubectl get jobs -A => which batch runs are incomplete or failing
  - kubectl logs job/db-migrate => why the last attempt failed
failure_modes:
  - non-idempotent task retried after a partial success
  - backoffLimit exceeded, so the Job fails silently until somebody looks
related:
  - k8s.cron-job
  - design.idempotency
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/job/
